README -- what was changed in tet, and whyCMDS/GAMES/tet draws its board and never sees a keystroke.  REBUILT/tet.unixlibis this source rebuilt with three faults fixed.  Whether that is enough is aplay-test question; see notes/PLAYTEST-QUEUE.md.THREE FAULTS, STACKED.  Each one hid the next.1. THE KEYBOARD CODE WAS NOT COMPILED IN.  tet.c sets raw mode and reopens   stdin inside `#ifndef OSK'.  The OS-9 build got the #else branch, which is   `srand(0);' and nothing else -- no terminal setup at all.  Building with   that path enabled is the first step, and it needs LIB/unix.l, whose   ioctl.c implements TCGETA/TCSETA over _ss_opt.2. LIB/unix.l's ioctl HAD NO TCSETAW CASE.  tet goes raw with   `ioctl(1, TCSETAW, &tty)', and unixlib implemented TCSETA but not TCSETAW.   The call fell through silently, so the terminal kept echoing and the   program saw nothing.  SRC/unixlib/ioctl.c now sends TCSETAW and TCSETAF   into the TCSETA case -- OS-9's _ss_opt takes effect at once, so the   "after output drains" distinction does not arise.  LIB/unix.l was rebuilt.   That fix is not tet's alone: any program here setting terminal modes the   usual way was hitting the same silent no-op.3. IT REOPENED STDIN WITH NO ACCESS.  This is the one that actually stops the   reads:       close (0);       open (ttnam, O_NDELAY);   O_NDELAY is a Unix open() FLAG.  OS-9's open() takes an ACCESS MODE, and   DEFS/os9lib/fcntl.h defines O_NDELAY as 0 -- no read, no write.  So tet   closed its own input and reopened it unreadable.  Writes on fd 1 still   worked, which is exactly why the board draws and no key ever arrives.   Changed to S_IREAD.AND ONE CONSEQUENCE OF FIXING 3.  GetKey() loops `while read(0,...) != 0',which needs a read that returns 0 when nothing is waiting.  That is whatO_NDELAY was for on Unix, and OS-9 has no equivalent flag on a path -- ablocking read would hang the game between keystrokes and the piece wouldnever fall.  OS-9 asks first instead, so GetKey now calls _gs_rdy(0) for thecount of characters waiting and returns when it is zero.ALSO NEEDED.  randint.c, a two-line shim.  tet's makefile links/dd/lib/rand.r for randint(), and that object is on no disk here and in noarchive in the pool; the name survives only inside a comment inSRC/shuffle/xrand.c, whose own function is called rnd_ri.  Same file astools/rebuild/shims/os9randint.c.BUILDING    cc tet.c MoveR.c MoveL.c NewP.c AdvanceP.c Rotate.c randint.c \       -qm=16k -n=tet -f=R_tet \       -l=/dd/LIB/unix.l -l=/dd/LIB/curses.l -l=/dd/LIB/termlib.l \       -l=/dd/LIB/math.l  Note there is no -DOSK: the point is to compile the non-OSK branch.